course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 13
title: Granny's "a little more", festival food — and "come on!"
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ukrainian grannies play a polite game: they offer shche trOshky — a little more — again and again, often three times, and you answer kindly and firmly ni, DYAkuyu, ya nayIvsya.
explain: The magic key for asking is mozhna …? — may I …? — and with BUD laska added it is perfect: mozhna vodu, BUD laska?
explain: Festival food has its own words: kutYa, sweet wheat with poppy seeds, honey and nuts, on Christmas Eve; paska, the tall iced Easter bread; and Granny's pyRIzhky, little filled buns, any day.
explain: Then you run outside, and the playground words start: daVAI! (come on!), hayDA! (off we go!), lovY! (catch!), kydAi! (throw it!), and tvIy khid — your move.
fact: shche trOshky means a little more, Granny offering more food; trOshky means a little bit.
fact: mozhna ...? means may I have ...? or can I ...?, the polite way to ask for anything, and the answer mozhna means yes, you may.
fact: pyRIzhky are little baked or fried buns with a filling, from Granny's baking or a picnic basket; pirozhki is the Russian word.
fact: kutYa is sweet wheat with poppy seeds, honey and nuts, eaten at the Christmas Eve dinner; tasting the first spoon is part of the evening.
fact: paska is a tall Easter bread with white icing; the festival itself is called Velykden.
fact: daVAI! means come on!, let's go! or go on!, the all-purpose word for urging a friend; in casual Russian-speaking talk it can also mean bye.
fact: hayDA! means let's go! or off we go!, with a cheerful old Ukrainian feel.
fact: nu! means well? or come on!, hurrying a friend; said with a long sigh it means «oh, come on…», disappointed.
fact: lovY! means catch!, when throwing a ball; it is the same verb as catching fish, lovyty rybu, and has nothing to do with «love».
fact: kydAi! means throw it!, asking for the ball.
fact: tvIy khid means your move, in board games and card games; the kh is said like the ch in Scottish «loch».
fact: tvoya chErha means your turn in a queue, for example for the slide; chErha means queue or turn.
exercise: What does shche trOshky mean? | A little more | That's enough | Too much | Eat it all now
exercise: Granny offers more for the third time and you're full. What fits best? | ni, DYAkuyu, ya nayIvsya, said kindly | nE-a! and push the plate | Say nothing and leave | Eat it all and feel sick
exercise: What does mozhna ...? mean? | May I ...? / can I ...? | Must I ...? / do I have to ...? | Where is ...? | Why ...?
exercise: How do you make mozhna vodu? perfectly polite? | Add BUD laska | Add nu! | Add daVAI! | Shout it twice
exercise: What are pyRIzhky? | Little baked or fried buns with filling | Thin pancakes | Small pies made of ice cream, wrapped in wafers | Cabbage rolls
exercise: What is kutYa? | Sweet wheat with poppy seeds, honey and nuts | A Christmas tree | A Christmas cake with white icing and dried fruit | A hot fruit drink
exercise: When is kutYa eaten? | On Christmas Eve | At every breakfast | At Easter only, with eggs | On birthdays, with candles
exercise: What is paska? | A tall Easter bread with white icing | The name of the Easter festival itself | A painted Easter egg | Pasta
exercise: What is the Easter festival itself called? | Velykden | paska | kutYa | Rizdvo
exercise: What does daVAI! mean between kids? | Come on! let's go! | Give it back to me now! | Goodbye for ever! | Be quiet!
exercise: What does hayDA! mean? | Let's go! off we go! | Hide! quick, everyone hide! | Stop right there, now! | Hi there, my old mate!
exercise: What does nu! said with a long sigh mean? | Oh, come on… (disappointed) | Hooray! | Thank you! | Well done, that was brilliant!
exercise: What does lovY! mean? | Catch! | I love you! | Throw it! | Low down!
exercise: What does kydAi! mean? | Throw it! | Kick it! | Keep it! | Catch it!
exercise: What does tvIy khid mean in a board game? | Your move | Your new hat | My turn now | The game is over
exercise: How do you say the kh in khid? | Like the ch in Scottish «loch» | Like k in «kid» | Like h in «hat», soft and breathy | Silent
exercise: Where would you hear tvoya chErha? | In a queue for the slide | At the end of a film | At the dinner table only | In a shop selling cherries
test: «lovy! means love, she fancies you» Which is right? | No, lovY! means catch! — the ball is coming | Yes, it means I love you | Yes, it's a song | No, it means go away and leave her alone, she's busy
test: Granny: «shche trOshky?» — «ni, DYAkuyu». Granny: «nu, shche trOshky!» What is going on? | The polite offering game; say it kindly and firmly again | She is angry with you | She didn't hear; shout | She wants you to cook
test: Sashko is holding the ball, you want it. What do you shout? | kydAi! | lovY! | tvIy khid | shche trOshky
test: You threw the ball to Sashko. What did you shout as you threw? | lovY! | kydAi! | tvoya chErha | mozhna?
test: You lose the card game and Sashko groans «nuuuu…». What does he mean? | Oh come on… — he's disappointed | He's very happy and wants to play again | He's saying hello | He's asking for water
test: It's Christmas Eve and the first dish passed round is kutYa. What would happen if you refused to even taste it? | You'd miss a ritual part of the evening; trying one spoon is expected | Nothing, it's just a snack | Everyone would cheer | They'd give you paska instead
test: «paska is Easter, the whole holiday» Which is right? | No, paska is the bread; the feast is Velykden | Yes, paska is the holiday | paska is Christmas | paska is a painted egg, and the bread is called Velykden
test: «daVAI! means bye» says a kid who speaks Russian at home. What is true? | In casual Russian talk it can mean bye, but here it's mostly come on! | It never means anything but bye | It means catch | It means thank you
test: At chess Sashko taps the board and says «nu! tvIy khid». What should you do? | Make your move | Get up and leave | Throw the ball | Say ya nayIvsya
test: In the queue for the slide a kid says «tvoya chErha». What happens next? | You go down the slide | You wait longer at the back | You leave the queue and go home | You give him a sweet to pass
test: «pyrizhky, pirozhki, whatever» What is right? | pyRIzhky is the Ukrainian word; pirozhki is Russian | They are completely different foods, one sweet and one meaty | pirozhki is the Ukrainian word | pyRIzhky are biscuits
test: «mozhna?» you ask, pointing at the last pyRIzhok. Granny says «mozhna!». What did she say? | Yes, you may | No, you may not | Ask your mum | Wait for Christmas
exam: Identify the expression by which a child courteously requests permission to have or to do something. | mozhna ...?, preferably accompanied by BUD laska | daVAI! | nu! | kydAi!
exam: Ascertain the occasion upon which kutYa is customarily partaken of. | The dinner of Christmas Eve | The morning of Easter Sunday | Every ordinary breakfast | The celebration of a birthday
exam: Select the response consistent with the established distinction between paska and Velykden. | paska denotes the Easter bread, whereas Velykden denotes the festival itself | paska denotes the festival, whereas Velykden denotes the bread | Both denote painted eggs | Both denote the Christmas Eve dinner
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the repeated offer shche trOshky made by a grandmother? | It forms part of a customary courtesy, to be answered with a kind but firm refusal when one is satisfied | It constitutes a command that must be obeyed | It signifies that the meal has concluded | It is an expression of displeasure
